nodecode-doc-markdown is a rewrite of TakeshiTGAL's TakeshiTGAL/hermes-plugin-doc-markdown:

  https://github.com/TakeshiTGAL/hermes-plugin-doc-markdown
  commit 4206b7f8066fe84f2c295f480a441f47154d9315
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 TakeshiTGAL (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's one tool, doc_convert, and the path guard
around it, in Common Lisp. The guard is the original's, judgement included:
an absolute path only, no http/https/file URL in any letter case, every
symbolic link followed before anything is judged, a path component that
starts with "." refused, the harness's home refused, an allowlist of
extensions, a size cap read from the config and never from the call, and a
refusal that names what to do next (error_type and next_step, here one
line of text). The extension allowlist, the 20 MiB default, the 200000
character default cut and the "the worker is not a sandbox" posture are the
original's too.

The Python runtime is gone. The original handed a file to Microsoft's
MarkItDown (MIT) in a child interpreter; here a .docx, .xlsx or .pptx is read
as the zip of OOXML parts it is, with a DEFLATE decoder (RFC 1951) and a zip
central-directory reader written in the cell, and an HTML page, a CSV, JSON
and plain text are read directly. No MarkItDown source, and no Python, node
or npm, is bundled or run.

What was left out, and why:
  - .xls (BIFF) workbooks: MarkItDown read them best-effort through a Python
    library; a BIFF reader is a format of its own, so the rewrite refuses .xls
    and says to save it as .xlsx or .csv.
  - PDF is best-effort. Every stream is inflated where its bytes say zlib and
    the text operators (Tj, TJ) are read out; a scanned page, or text in
    compressed object streams, answers nothing and refuses instead of
    answering noise. MarkItDown used pdfminer, which does the full job.
  - The child process, its 8-second and 60-second timeouts and its 880 MiB
    memory cap: there is no child. The conversion runs in this process and the
    section's caps are max_file_bytes and max_output_chars.
  - The plugin's pluggable third-party MarkItDown plugins, its LLM caption and
    transcription switches and its (unregistered) web extract provider: v0.1 of
    the original had none of them wired either.

What the rewrite adds: the section doc-markdown (max_file_bytes,
max_output_chars, refuse_hidden), the /doc command for the operator, and
(help :doc-markdown).
